%N A077055 Number of distinct meander shapes with n crossings of a line.
%C A077055 From _Hunter Hogan_, Nov 17 2026: (Start)
%C A077055 A meander, also called an open meander, is a self-avoiding infinite curve that crosses an infinite line n times and whose "left" side is "above" the line.
%C A077055 Two meanders have the same meander shape if one is the reverse, complement, or reverse complement of the other. Two meanders are reverses if n is odd and they cross the line in opposite orders. Two meanders are complementary if they cross the line n times, n is odd, and the sum of each ordered pair of crossings is n + 1. Finally, two meanders are reverse complements if they cross the line n times and the reverse of one meander is the complement of the other meander.
%C A077055 An open meander is equivalent to a strip of stamps folded into a stack, but neither the first stamp nor the last stamp can be tucked in between two other stamps. (End)
%H A077055 Hunter Hogan, <a href="/A077055/b077055.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..59</a> (terms 1..43 from Stéphane Legendre).
%H A077055 Iaroslav Argunov, <a href="https://github.com/Kimargin8/meander-enumerations/releases/download/v2026.11.18/symmetric.pdf">Symmetric open meanders with two marked ends</a> (2026).
%H A077055 Iaroslav Argunov, <a href="https://github.com/Kimargin8/meander-enumerations/blob/v2026.11.18/oeis/A077055-crossings.txt">Exact open shape counts by physical crossing number, including odd lengths through 75</a> (2026).
